---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import MouseDpiTool from '../../components/MouseDpiTool.astro';
import NewToolRelatedTests from '../../components/NewToolRelatedTests.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import JsonLd from '../../components/seo/JsonLd.astro';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../../config';

const description = 'Calculate effective mouse DPI from a distance you measure and the pixels traveled. eDPI needs both DPI and sensitivity. The browser cannot read sensor DPI.';
const faq = [
  { question: 'Does this effective mouse DPI test read the sensor?', answer: 'No. The browser cannot auto-read real hardware or sensor DPI. The figure is CSS pixels of pointer travel divided by a physical distance you measure. OS cursor settings and display scaling are inside that result. This is not a sensor specification reading.' },
  { question: 'When does this page show eDPI?', answer: 'eDPI is the DPI you type multiplied by the sensitivity you type. Both boxes have to contain a positive finite number. If either box is empty, the eDPI line stays blank. The page does not guess the missing factor, and it does not copy the measured effective DPI into that formula unless you typed it yourself.' },
  { question: 'Why can the same hand movement produce a different number?', answer: 'Pointer speed, enhance-pointer-precision options, raw-input settings in a game, and display scaling all change how far the cursor travels on screen. This page counts that on-screen result for the distance you measured. It does not recover an internal count from the mouse.' },
  { question: 'Which distance units are accepted?', answer: 'Inches, centimeters, and millimeters. A centimeter value is divided by 2.54, and a millimeter value is divided by 25.4, because the international inch is exactly 25.4 millimeters. A missing, zero, or negative distance does not produce an effective DPI.' },
  { question: 'Can the pixel count be typed instead of dragged?', answer: 'Yes. The pad writes the pointer-path length into the on-screen travel field, rounded to a hundredth of a CSS pixel. You can replace that number with a count you made on the 50-pixel marks or with any other count you trust. Calculate uses the number in the field. A blank, zero, or negative pixel count does not become a DPI.' },
];
const related = [
  { href: '/mouse-test', title: 'Mouse Test', description: 'Check buttons, movement, and wheel input.', icon: '◉' },
  { href: '/mouse-polling-rate-test', title: 'Polling Rate Test', description: 'Estimate pointer-event timing, a different question.', icon: '⌁' },
  { href: '/double-click-test', title: 'Double Click Test', description: 'Look at unexpected repeat clicks.', icon: '⠿' },
  { href: '/reaction-time-test', title: 'Reaction Time Test', description: 'Time a click after a visual cue.', icon: '◌' },
];
const schema = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: 'Effective Mouse DPI Test', url: absoluteUrl('/mouse-dpi-test'), applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any', offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' } },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faq.map((item) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: item.question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.answer } })) },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Mouse DPI Test: Effective On-Screen DPI" {description} path="/mouse-dpi-test">
  <JsonLd data={schema} />
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Mouse DPI Test' }]} />
  <section class="page-hero shell compact">
    <h1>Effective Mouse DPI Test</h1>
    <p>Divide CSS-pixel pointer travel by a physical distance you measure. The browser does not read the sensor.</p>
  </section>
  <MouseDpiTool />
  <article class="content">
    <h2>How to use this effective mouse DPI test</h2>
    <p>Measure how far your hand actually moved the mouse, then tell the page how far the pointer traveled on screen. Put the physical distance in the first field and choose inches, centimeters, or millimeters. Put the on-screen travel in CSS pixels in the third field. Press Calculate. The effective DPI line is that pixel count divided by the distance after it has been converted to inches. Reset test clears the fields, the drag path, and every figure so the next try starts empty.</p>
    <p>The pad under the fields is a calibration surface, not a sensor probe. Drag across it and the page adds up the straight segments between pointer positions, then writes that length into the travel field. The faint marks are 50 CSS pixels apart, so you can also count a straight horizontal drag yourself and type that count. The marks are a ruler for this page’s CSS pixels. They are not millimeters printed on the mouse, and they are not a reading from the sensor inside it. If you already counted pixels another way, type that count and skip the drag. Calculate always uses the number sitting in the field at the moment you press the button.</p>
    <p>Leave DPI and sensitivity empty when you only want the measured quotient. Fill both only when you want eDPI as well. eDPI on this page means the DPI number you typed times the sensitivity number you typed. One filled box and one empty box does not produce an eDPI. A zero, a negative number, or a word in either box is an error for that box, and the eDPI line stays blank.</p>
    <p>If a required measurement is missing or unusable, the effective DPI line stays an em dash. The status line says which field failed. The page does not substitute a typical mouse setting, a previous visit, or a round number of its own. A click on the pad that does not move, a path that rounds to 0.00 CSS pixels, and a distance of zero are all refused in the same way.</p>

    <h2>What pixels divided by inches means here</h2>
    <p>The formula is fixed. Effective DPI equals on-screen travel in CSS pixels divided by the physical distance in inches. Two inches of hand movement and 1,600 CSS pixels of pointer travel produce 800, because 1,600 divided by 2 is 800. That sentence is arithmetic about the two numbers you entered. It is not a statement that a mouse reported 800, and it is not a rating of the device. The same pair of entries always produces the same quotient. Changing either entry changes the quotient in the obvious direction: more pixels over the same distance raise it, and a longer physical distance for the same pixels lowers it.</p>
    <p>Use the distance that matches the question you are asking. If you slid the mouse along a ruler on the desk, the inches are hand travel, and the quotient says how many CSS pixels the pointer moved per inch of that hand travel. Pointer speed and display scaling are inside that number, which is why this page calls it effective or on-screen DPI. If instead you laid a ruler on the monitor and counted CSS pixels across a known length of the glass, the same division describes that screen measurement. Say which distance you measured when you write the number down. The page cannot tell a desk ruler from a screen ruler, because both arrive as a distance you typed.</p>
    <p>A very small positive pair is still divided. One billionth of an inch and two billionths of a CSS pixel are a quotient of 2, and the page keeps that positive result rather than printing a made-up zero. A true zero is different. Zero distance and zero pixels are rejected before any division, because dividing by zero is undefined and a zero-pixel path is not a measurement of movement. Negative numbers are rejected too. They are not silently flipped to a positive DPI.</p>

    <h2>Why the browser cannot read sensor DPI</h2>
    <p>A mouse sensor counts motion in its own steps, often discussed as counts per inch. Those steps are handled by the mouse firmware, the operating system, and any pointer-speed setting long before a web page runs. The page never sees that register. It sees pointer events. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/uievents/#set-pointerlock-attributes-for-mousemove" rel="noopener noreferrer">UI Events specification</a> sets <code>movementX</code> and <code>movementY</code> from the difference in screen coordinates between mousemove events. That difference is a coordinate delta. It is not a query of the sensor’s counts-per-inch value, and this page does not pretend to reverse it back into one.</p>
    <p>The drag pad uses the same kind of information: each pointer position’s <code>clientX</code> and <code>clientY</code>, then the straight-line distance from the previous position. Adding those segments gives the path length in CSS pixels. A path that zigzags is longer than the straight line from the first point to the last, and the field shows the path length, not the shortcut. One point, or a press that never moves, has length zero. Non-finite coordinates are ignored by the calculation module rather than turned into a DPI. Nothing in that chain opens the USB device, reads a HID report, or asks the operating system for the mouse’s current DPI mode.</p>
    <p>Vendor software can still show a DPI or CPI number that came from the mouse. This page is not that software. If the two numbers disagree, both can be “right” about different questions. The vendor number is whatever that app read or last sent to the device. The number here is pixels of pointer travel divided by a distance you measured, with the current cursor settings left in place. Quote them separately. Do not paste this page’s quotient into a product review as if it were the sensor specification.</p>

    <h2>Inches, centimeters, and the 25.4 millimeter rule</h2>
    <p>The form accepts three units so you can type the ruler you actually used. Internally every distance becomes inches before the division. One inch stays one inch. Centimeters are divided by 2.54. Millimeters are divided by 25.4. The <a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/si-units-length" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST length-unit notes</a> record the international inch as exactly 25.4 millimeters, from the 1959 yard definition. Because of that exact factor, 25.4 millimeters and 800 CSS pixels are the same quotient as 1 inch and 800 CSS pixels. 2.54 centimeters and 800 CSS pixels match it as well. A 10 millimeter entry does not get rounded to an inch first; it is divided by 25.4 and then used at full precision.</p>
    <p>The unit menu does not guess. An empty unit or a word such as “inch” is an error, and no effective DPI is shown. The menu on this page only offers the three supported values, so a normal visit will not hit that error unless the field is cleared by the browser. The inches line under the result is the converted length, rounded for reading to a ten-thousandth of an inch. The quotient itself uses the unrounded conversion. If you are checking the arithmetic by hand, divide pixels by the exact inch value, not by a shortened decimal you copied from the inches line.</p>
    <p>Choose the unit before you press Calculate, and keep it with your notes. A “2” that meant centimeters is a different measurement from a “2” that meant inches. Reset test restores the unit menu to inches along with the empty fields. Look at the menu after a reset before you type the next distance, especially if the previous try used millimeters.</p>

    <h2>Cursor speed, scaling, and CSS pixels</h2>
    <p>Operating-system pointer speed sits between the sensor and the cursor. A faster setting moves the pointer farther on screen for the same hand movement, so the same desk distance produces more CSS pixels and a higher effective DPI. A slower setting does the opposite. “Enhance pointer precision” and similar acceleration options can make the quotient depend on how quickly you moved, not only on how far. For a comparison, move at a similar pace, use a straight slide, and do not change the pointer setting between runs. This page cannot turn acceleration off. It can only divide the travel you measured under the settings you left on.</p>
    <p>Display scaling changes the relationship between what a ruler shows on the glass and what the page calls a CSS pixel. The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-values-3/#absolute-lengths" rel="noopener noreferrer">CSS Values definition of absolute lengths</a> says that, on screen media, the pixel is the usual anchor unit and the physical units might not match their physical measurements. A CSS inch on the monitor is therefore not something this page treats as your desk ruler. The pixels in the formula are CSS pixels from pointer coordinates in this tab. They already include the browser’s scaling. The page does not multiply by <code>devicePixelRatio</code> and then call the product a hardware DPI. Doing that would invent a second number the sensor did not report.</p>
    <p>CSS also has a resolution unit spelled <code>dpi</code>, defined in the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/css-values-3/#resolution" rel="noopener noreferrer">resolution-units section</a> as dots per CSS inch of the canvas. That unit describes media resolution in style sheets. It is not the mouse sensor’s counts per inch, and this tool does not read it. When you see “DPI” in the optional eDPI fields, it means the number you type. When you see “effective DPI” in the result, it means pixels of pointer travel divided by your measured inches. Keep those labels apart from a monitor’s pixel density and from a stylesheet resolution.</p>

    <h2>eDPI only from the two factors you type</h2>
    <p>Players sometimes multiply a mouse DPI setting by an in-game sensitivity and call the product eDPI. This page will do that multiplication, and only that multiplication, when both factors are present. The worked example used by the formula is 800 times 0.5, which is 400. Those are sample inputs, not a measured mouse and not a recommended setting. If you type 800 in the DPI box and 0.5 in the sensitivity box, the eDPI line shows 400. If you type only 800, the eDPI line stays blank and the status says the missing factor was not guessed. Sensitivity alone is treated the same way. The page will not assume a sensitivity of 1 to fill the gap.</p>
    <p>The measured effective DPI is a separate line. It does not become the DPI factor unless you type it into the DPI box yourself. That separation matters because the measured quotient already includes operating-system pointer speed. Multiplying it again by a game sensitivity can be a comparison you want, but it is a second choice you have to type. The tool will not chain the two calculations on its own. An invalid factor, including a blank that is not really blank of meaning such as a word, a negative sign, or a zero, blocks eDPI even when the other factor is a perfectly ordinary number. The effective DPI line can still show a quotient from the distance and the pixels when those required inputs are valid.</p>
    <p>There is no stored profile and no account. Closing the tab drops the numbers. Nothing here is sent as a hardware identification, and the figures are not a score. Search interest in this kind of test was not measured for this page, so the page does not claim a ranking or a volume of users.</p>

    <h2>Repeat a measurement without overclaiming</h2>
    <p>For a desk measurement, pick a straight edge you can read, such as a ruler or a marked strip of paper. Note the start and end of the mouse body, not the shaking of your wrist. Reset test, type the distance, drag once across the pad or type the pixel count, and press Calculate. Then reset and do it again. Write down both quotients and the pointer-speed setting you left untouched. A single run that bumped the mouse, changed windows, or included a lift in the middle of the drag is a weak note. The path length includes every segment the browser delivered, so a slip sideways makes the pixel count larger than the straight desk distance and pushes the quotient up.</p>
    <p>Compare one change at a time. If you edit pointer speed, repeat the same physical distance and read the new pixel count. If you change a DPI switch on the mouse with its own software, this page still will not read that switch. You can measure again and see whether on-screen travel changed. A change that shows up under the same hand distance is a change in the pointer result. It is still not a copy of the number in the vendor app. If you need the mode the mouse was set to, read it where that mode is configured. Use this page for the on-screen arithmetic, and keep the disclaimer in view when you share the figure: results depend on your measurements, OS cursor settings, and display scaling.</p>
    <p>Very short drags are allowed when the numbers stay positive, but they are easy to mis-measure. A few pixels of travel over a few millimeters of hand movement can swing the quotient when your ruler is only accurate to a millimeter. Prefer a longer, clearly marked slide when you want a stable note. The tool will still calculate the short one honestly, and it will still refuse a zero.</p>

    <h2>Related mouse checks</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/mouse-test">Mouse Test</a> records buttons, wheel direction, and whether the pointer is moving. It does not divide that movement by a ruler. The <a href="/mouse-polling-rate-test">Polling Rate Test</a> estimates how often pointer events arrive, which is a timing question rather than a distance question. The <a href="/double-click-test">Double Click Test</a> looks at repeated clicks. The <a href="/reaction-time-test">Reaction Time Test</a> times a click after a cue. None of those pages reads a sensor DPI either. Use this page only for the pixel-per-inch quotient you just measured, and say so when you quote it.</p>
    <NewToolRelatedTests tests={related} />
    <FAQ items={faq} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
